    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scientists generally argue that mandatory vaccination is an ethical imperative to prevent vaccine-preventable diseases and protect the most vulnerable members of society who cannot be vaccinated. In contrast, civil liberties advocates often contend that any mandate infringes upon individual bodily autonomy and the Fourth Amendment right against unreasonable government interference. While proponents of mandates emphasize community safety and herd immunity over personal freedom, critics highlight the risk of coercion and suggest that public health policy should prioritize voluntary, well-informed choices whenever possible.",
